# ArgonOS - ESP32-C3 (RISC-V), the "C3 SuperMini" on the desk.
#
# The fourth chip this system builds for and the second RISC-V one, so the hard
# part was already paid for by the C6: everything above components/argon_port
# compiled for it unchanged.  What is different here is not the instruction set
# but the size of the machine.
#
# The board: ESP32-C3 (usually -MINI-1 or a bare chip), 4 MB flash, 400 KB HP
# SRAM, no PSRAM, ONE core at 160 MHz.  The Type-C socket goes to the chip
# itself, so the console is the built-in USB serial device and not a UART.  No
# display and no card slot: a header of thirteen pins and a green LED.
#
# QEMU DOES run this target, since 18 Sep 2026: `idf_tools.py install
# qemu-riscv32` and `argon target esp32c3-qemu`, which is this file plus
# sdkconfig.esp32c3.qemu.  What the emulator cannot show is the radio, the
# USB console and the pins; everything else no longer waits for the board.
#
# THE THREE NUMBERS THAT MAKE THIS BOARD DIFFERENT FROM THE C6:
#
#   400 KB of SRAM, not 512.  A hundred kilobytes is most of what Wi-Fi wants,
#   so the buffers below are smaller and the arena is smaller, and if something
#   refuses to start for want of memory those are the two lines to move.
#
#   One core.  Cluster threads still work - a thread is a thread - but "every
#   core of every board" means one core here, and `grind` will say so.
#
#   Wi-Fi 4, not 6, and BLE only.  Nothing above cares; it is written down so
#   that nobody looks for 802.11ax settings that this part has no symbols for.

CONFIG_IDF_TARGET="esp32c3"

# 4 MB, sharing the C6's table: the same chip-sized flash and the same reason
# for the split - C: has to be big enough to hold what gets flashed into it in
# one esptool run, because the console's hex protocol is minutes per kilobyte.
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_4MB=y
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHMODE_DIO=y
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHFREQ_80M=y
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_CUSTOM_FILENAME="partitions_c6.csv"

# 160 MHz is this part's ceiling, not a choice.
CONFIG_ESP_DEFAULT_CPU_FREQ_MHZ_160=y

# The console, and the one line that makes this board usable at all.
#
# There is no bridge chip: the USB-C socket is wired to GPIO 18 and 19, the
# chip's own USB pins.  Left on UART0 the system would be talking to GPIO 20
# and 21 - two pads on the header - and the cable in the socket would carry
# nothing.  The device is offered above as one more serial port, the one after
# the last real UART, and the console attaches to it by number like any other.
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG=y
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_UART_DEFAULT=n

# The code arena, out of the same heap Wi-Fi wants.  32 KB rather than the C6's
# 64: this part has a hundred kilobytes less to divide, and an arena that cannot
# be allocated is a board that does not boot rather than one that runs smaller
# programs.  Raise it if an application is refused for want of code space AND
# there is memory to spare at the boot line - both halves, not either.
#
# IT WAS 48, AND 48 IS THE VALUE THAT MAKES THIS BOARD UNABLE TO RUN ANYTHING.
#
# The arena is a static array in .iram.bss, so every kilobyte of it is a
# kilobyte the heap never sees - and on this part the heap is short enough that
# the SIZE OF THE LARGEST FREE BLOCK is what decides whether a program starts.
# At 48 KB, on the bench, with two wires up:
#
#   $ mem
#                    total        free     largest
#     internal       173 KB        23 KB        15 KB
#   $ run c:\nodehirv.axe
#   E proc: nodehirv.axe: no memory for a 16384 byte task stack
#           (internal SRAM and PSRAM both failed)
#
# 23 KB free and a 16 KB stack refused, because no single block was big enough.
# 48 KB of arena bought nothing: what is loaded into it is the wire driver
# (6.8 KB of code, 1.9 KB of data) and a program the size of TONE (19 KB), so a
# 32 KB ceiling holds both and hands 16 KB back to the heap at the same time -
# and this board's whole purpose in the cluster is to run the guest on the
# other end of a wire, so "cannot start a program" is "cannot do its job".
#
# 32 and not 16: TONE is on this board's C: on purpose (it has no converter, so
# a tone started here lands on the neighbour's), and it needs 19 KB of the
# arena beside the driver.  Sixteen would fit the stack and lose the program.
CONFIG_ARGON_APP_ARENA_KB=32

# And the one line without which the arena is useless.  It is not the C6's line,
# and finding that out cost the first boot on this board.
#
# The C6 turns off CONFIG_ESP_SYSTEM_PMP_IDRAM_SPLIT.  THAT SYMBOL DOES NOT
# EXIST ON THE C3, and kconfiglib shrugs at a name it does not know rather than
# complaining - so the line was there, read, and did nothing.  This part guards
# its memory with the PMS peripheral instead, behind a different name.
#
# What the board did without it, every boot, reproducibly:
#
#   E (18) boot: stage 'media' failed with -19
#   Guru Meditation Error: Core 0 panic'ed (Memory protection fault).
#   MEPC : 0x42019554   MCAUSE : 0x0000001a   MTVAL : 0xfef71ae3
#
# and 0x42019554 is copy_image() in the loader: the fault is on the WRITE of a
# module's code into the arena, not on the jump into it.  Three of those in a
# row put the system into recovery, where modules are skipped - so the symptom
# a reader meets first is "the wire driver did not load", which points nowhere
# near memory protection.
#
# ARGON_APP_ARENA_KB says in so many words that ESP-IDF hands out no memory that
# is both writable and executable through one address, and the arena is how
# ArgonOS gets some anyway.  It is a protection this design cannot keep - see
# [T-12] on the no-MMU trade - and not one it silently loses.
CONFIG_ESP_SYSTEM_MEMPROT_FEATURE=n
CONFIG_ESP_SYSTEM_MEMPROT_FEATURE_LOCK=n

# WI-FI OFF TOO, since 23 Sep 2026, at Maxim's word.  It was kept for one
# reason - the board sat on a power bank and telnet was its only console.  It
# is on USB now, beside the Zero, and what it is for is the wire: the stack
# held ~50 KB of a machine that had 25 KB free after boot with its largest
# piece 7 KB, which is less than one DMA ring for the UART at 16 Mbaud.
# Getting files on is mksysfs over USB, as on every other board here.
CONFIG_ARGON_ENABLE_NET=n
CONFIG_ARGON_NET_WIFI=n
CONFIG_ETH_USE_OPENETH=n

# BLE IS OFF ON THIS BOARD, and it is the 400 KB that decides it.
#
# Measured here with it on: 142 KB of heap in total, 95 KB of that held by the
# system, 46 KB free - and an application that asked for one 8 KB thread stack
# was refused (-12).  `grind` got as far as printing its one-core baseline and
# then said "nowhere to run anything", which on a board whose whole purpose is
# to be a second node is the same as not working.
#
# What this board is: a header, a wire and no glass.  It has no keyboard to
# pair, nothing to be a HID host for, and no user sitting at it - its user is
# on the other end of the wire.  Wi-Fi stays, because getting a file onto a
# board over the console is 0.6 KB/s and silently truncates.
#
# Turn it back on for a board that needs it, and expect to lower the arena in
# the same breath.
CONFIG_ARGON_ENABLE_BLE=n
CONFIG_BT_ENABLED=n

CONFIG_ARGON_NET_WIFI_MON=n

# Smaller than the C6's, and that is the 400 KB talking: these buffers come out
# of internal SRAM, which is the memory this part is short of.
CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_STATIC_RX_BUFFER_NUM=4
CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_DYNAMIC_RX_BUFFER_NUM=8
CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_TX_BUFFER_TYPE=1
CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_DYNAMIC_TX_BUFFER_NUM=8
CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_AMPDU_TX_ENABLED=n
CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_AMPDU_RX_ENABLED=n

# A panic reboots rather than halting: this target has no fault recovery (see
# fault_hw.c - the RISC-V half says so in words), so the panic path is the only
# path, and a machine that comes back and says "reset: panic" beats one that
# stops with nobody watching.
CONFIG_ESP_SYSTEM_PANIC_PRINT_REBOOT=y
CONFIG_ESP_SYSTEM_PANIC_PRINT_HALT=n

# No host helper on UART1 - and here that is not only about the 80 KB it costs.
# UART1 IS THE CLUSTER WIRE on this board.  HostFS would configure the same
# port for its own protocol and the two would talk over each other.
CONFIG_ARGON_ENABLE_HOSTFS=n

# No virtual panel: no QEMU runs this target, and the component that talks to
# the emulated one does not compile for RISC-V.
CONFIG_ARGON_PANEL_QEMU=n

# No DACs on this part at all, and the ADC is left off until something on the
# board needs measuring.
CONFIG_ARGON_ENABLE_AUDIO=n
CONFIG_ARGON_ENABLE_ADC=n

# There is no glass on this board, so the console grid is chosen for the thing
# that will actually read it: a terminal on the USB cable.  Eighty by twenty-
# five, the size every terminal already is.
CONFIG_ARGON_CONSOLE_COLS=80
CONFIG_ARGON_CONSOLE_ROWS=25

# Room for a neighbour's devices as well as this board's - and this board has
# TWO neighbours, so the pair's arithmetic does not cover it.  Measured on the
# bench: this board's own registry is twelve entries, the CYD offers fifteen
# (one of them its link, skipped) and the S3-Zero twelve.  Twelve plus fifteen
# plus twelve is thirty-nine.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, MEASURED on this board with `mem` either side of the change:
# thirty-two kilobyte entries is seven kilobytes - free went 39 -> 32 KB and the
# largest block 31 -> 25 KB - because a borrowed device costs a slot in this
# table *and* an entry in the proxy table behind it.  That is 224 bytes an
# entry, which is what the number below is now chosen against.
#
# SIXTY-FOUR BECAME FORTY-EIGHT, AND THE SECOND GUEST IS WHY.
#
# The C3's whole reason to exist in this cluster is to run a program on behalf
# of a neighbour, and the pair test now reaches the state the middle board has
# to survive: ONE guest already running (a migrated MIGRANT, or the neighbour's
# build being substituted for a foreign one) while a SECOND is asked for.  With
# the table at sixty-four entries the C3 has 15308 bytes free and a 7424-byte
# largest block while that one guest runs, and the second is refused - by 768
# bytes, which is what a 224-byte entry times sixteen is worth.  At forty-eight
# the same state has room for the second 8 KB stack, and thirty-nine is still
# what it needs, with nine to spare.
#
# Forty was the value that first suggested itself here and it is the wrong one:
# thirty-nine is the measured requirement with NO margin, and a table that fills
# up refuses the screen and the touchscreen first (see the note that follows).
# Forty-eight is the smallest round number that holds what the middle must hold.
#
# THE OLD VALUE HERE WAS FORTY, AND IT WAS ALSO A LINE THAT DID NOTHING.  See
# argon/device.h: the header read CONFIG_ARGON_DEV_MAX without asking the port
# for it, so every board ran on the twenty-four in its #else - and this board
# refused the CYD's screen, touchscreen and card slot with -ENFILE (-23) while
# its sdkconfig said forty.  A number here is only worth writing now that it
# arrives.
CONFIG_ARGON_DEV_MAX=48

# The timer task at 2 KB, not 4.  Its one client on this board is the kernel's
# lock watch (ag_port_lockwatch_start: a line a second at most, formatted into
# 160 bytes), there is no radio here to add others, and measured it had used
# 225 bytes of the 4089 it was given - on a board where the merge of 25 Sep 2026
# had just cost twelve kilobytes of heap.
CONFIG_ESP_TIMER_TASK_STACK_SIZE=2048
